  "query_preview": "..Next chance for showers and some storms returns\nThursday night through Friday with sharpening nw flow bringing \nshortwave/front through. More consensus with this in the models \ncompared to 24hr ago, so pops have ramped up to near 60% over \nnorthern areas. Better instability still southwest of the state, \nbut could be sufficient enough return flow ahead of front to lead\nto stronger storms. Though storm predictability is low this far \nout, Machine learning guidance continues to paint broad 5 to 15% \nprob severe, so will continue to monitor. Behind the front,\nimagine Saturday could be a day with plenty of clouds and some \nscattered instability showers given the broad troughing settling \nacross the Great Lakes. It then turns drier for Sunday as high \npressure moves in. Nothing too notable in terms of temperatures \nwith highs in the upper 70s to lower 80s much of this week, \ntrending more solidly into the 70s next weekend. \n\nEven though we do have at least some rain chances for the week, \nno widespread rain chances are expected given this kind of splitting\npattern we are stuck in, with stronger jet dynamics staying north\nand greatest instability gradient persisting south. We could \ncertainly use some rain. Rainfall deficits in the last 30 days \nare now running over 3 inches for much of north-central WI, with \nmainly 1 to 2 inch elsewhere. As of early July not even 50% of our \nforecast area was in a D0 (Abnormally Dry) classification. As of \nthe latest drought monitor (8/11), over 90% of our area is within \nD1 (Moderate Drought), while parts of Oneida and Vilas counties \nare into D2 (Severe Drought).",
